hing in between. Don't miss it. What happens when you buy crypto? Behind the screen is a powerful system called an exchange, where your orders become real trades. There are two types of exchanges, centralized SECs like Binance or Coinbase and decentralized DECs like Uniswap. Both have pros and cons. In a centralized exchange, your crypto is managed by the platform. You trade within their system, and they handle custody. Decentralized exchanges run on blockchain. You keep control of your funds and trade via smart contracts, no middleman. Every trade starts with an order, market, or limit. Market buys instantly at the current price, while limit waits for your target. Order books match buyers and sellers. Think of it as a digital marketplace where prices shift every second. Liquidity matters. High liquidity means faster trades and tighter spreads. Low liquidity can lead to slippage and worse prices. Trades are the price difference between buying and selling. Exchanges profit from spreads and trading fees. Fees vary. Centralized exchanges charge trading and withdrawal fees. Decentralized ones require gas fees paid in ETH or similar. To use DEXs, you need a wallet like Metamask. You connect it to the exchange and approve each transaction. Slippage happens when the final trade price is different from expected. It's common in volatile markets or low liquidity. Order versus taker. Makers place limit orders, takers fill them. Exchanges reward makers for adding liquidity. Staking spot trading margin futures. Modern exchanges offer many features, but each comes with different risks. Margin trading allows you to borrow funds to amplify trades, but high rewards come with high risks. Losses can multiply. KYC is mandatory in many centralized exchanges. You must verify your identity to trade larger amounts or withdraw funds. On DEXs, there's no KYC you trade anonymously using wallets, but it also means no customer support or recourse. Flash crashes and manipulation can occur. In smaller exchanges or low-volume tokens, whales can move prices fast. Listing a token on an exchange increases visibility and liquidity, but shady projects also launch tokens, do your own research. Exchanges also hold reserves. If they go bankrupt or get hacked, user funds may be lost, like MT, GOX, or FTX. Security matters. Enable 2FA, use strong passwords, and avoid storing large amounts on exchanges. DYOR always research the exchange. Reputation, history, support, and fees all matter. Don't blindly trust the shiny interface. New models are emerging. Hybrid exchanges combine the ease of CEX with the security of DEX. Trading bots are used by pros to automate strategies, but for beginners, they can be dangerous if not understood well. The future of exchanges, AI-powered trading, instant cross-chain swaps, and on-chain reputation systems. In the end, exchanges are your gateway to the crypto world. Learn the systems, use caution, and always control your risk. Stablecoins are cryptocurrencies designed to hold steady value, often pegged to a fiat currency like the U.S. dollar, but are they truly stable or is it just branding? Unlike Bitcoin, stablecoins don't fluctuate wildly. That makes them useful for trading, payments, and storing value in volatile crypto markets. There are three main types of stablecoins, fiat-backed, crypto-backed, and algorithmic. Each works differently and carries different risks. Fiat-backed stablecoins like USDT or USDC are backed by real money in bank accounts. For every coin, there's supposed to be one real dollar. Fiat-backed stablecoins like DAI are backed by crypto-collateral like Ethereum, but use over-collateralization to maintain value. Algorithmic stablecoins rely on code and supply-demand mechanics to stay stable, but when it fails, collapse happens fast. TeruSD was an algorithmic stablecoin that crashed in 2022, wiping out billions and damaging trust. A harsh reminder that stable doesn't mean safe. Why use stablecoins? They're great for